ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: BM25-only lexical retrieval operators debugging unexplained rank dips when distractor documents acquire shared topical boilerplate containing query terms while the canonical answer stays clean; and RAG teams seeking evidence that normal corpus-hygiene template drift does not threaten dense retrieval rank stability.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only for the broad retrieval claim. L1 (EXP-0048) shows realistic query-bearing templates produce flat rank 1.00 (CI ±0.00) at every template length L for both bge-base-en-v1.5 and e5-base-v2; interior-peak test not triggered; score-margin dips slightly (+0.234→+0.138→+0.226 for bge; +0.098→+0.065→+0.101 for e5) then recovers, never negative. For BM25, the inverted-U is real and mechanistically clean—b=0 abolishes recovery (monotone 1.10→14.30, 0/20 seeds), b>0 restores it (20/20 seeds at b≥0.25), BM25L (Lv-Zhai 2011, delta=0.5) persists the pattern (3/4 cells, 20/20 seeds strongest)—but the recovery arm is pivoted length normalization (Singhal-Buckley-Mitra 1996) made visible via competitor templating, not a new operational control.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [BM25-only deployments are a minority in modern RAG where dense or hybrid dominates; the effect requires query-term-bearing templates on distractors with the answer untouched—an adversarial or data-quality edge case, not routine hygiene drift; standard mitigations (b>0, BM25L) already deployed; L1 demonstrates dense models are immune under realistic conditions, reducing urgency; operational monitoring of template length vs. rank is not a standard metric and would require custom instrumentation]
RATIONALE: Packet L0 (EXP-0044 + remediation EXP-0047) establishes the BM25-specific inverted-U with b-sweep proof that length normalization is necessary and BM25L robustness, confirming the mechanism. Packet L1 (EXP-0048) is the load-bearing new fact: under realistic templates dense retrieval does not exhibit the inverted-U; only adversarial verbatim-query injection displaces the clean answer, and then model-dependently (bge monotone-worse to rank 101, e5 partial inverted-U 1→13.5→1.00 via 512-token truncation/mean-pool dilution, not BM25 length-norm). The general corpus-hygiene risk framing FAILED ecological validity. The surviving BM25-only finding is valid but narrow and restates known length-norm dynamics. The L1 negative result is actionable guidance: dense RAG operators can deprioritize hygiene-driven rank-collapse concerns and instead focus on adversarial injection robustness; BM25 operators gain a diagnostic explanation for transient rank dips but no new mitigation beyond existing length-norm controls.
